## The mechanisms I think you are still under-specifying

The biggest ones are these.

### 1. Mechanical baseline

This is central enough to deserve its own full protocol.

You need to define:

```text
What is neutral?
How many runs?
What seed set?
Are policies completely disabled?
Is standardized control used?
What information is revealed?
Can agents inspect raw trajectories?
Can they inspect opponent body parameters?
How is uncertainty reported?
```

A proper output:

```json
{
  "matchup": ["boris-body-v4", "morty-body-v2"],
  "ruleset": "baseline-v1",
  "runs": 1000,
  "seed_set": "public-baseline-2026-s01",
  "results": {
    "boris": 0.31,
    "morty": 0.62,
    "draw": 0.07
  },
  "metrics": {
    "collision_advantage": {},
    "spin_endurance": {},
    "center_stability": {},
    "rim_vulnerability": {}
  }
}
```

Then this becomes **public evidence**, not just a warm-up round.

---

### 2. Information model

This is huge and not fully nailed down.

You need to specify exactly what each agent knows.

There should be explicit classes:

```text
PUBLIC BEFORE MATCH

arena
rules
body geometry
body public stats
historical matches
mechanical baseline
public rankings

PRIVATE BEFORE MATCH

own internal memory
own opponent model
own policy history

OBSERVABLE DURING MATCH

positions
velocities
spin
energy?
integrity?
ability state?
opponent internal state? NO
```

This determines the actual game theory.

Without this document you don't really know whether RoboBladez is:

```text
perfect information
imperfect information
partial observation
```

or some hybrid.

---

### 3. Policy timing semantics

You need an exact definition of:

```text
tick rate
observation time
policy decision time
action application time
physics resolution time
```

Something like:

```text
STATE(t)

↓ freeze state

Observation_A(t)
Observation_B(t)

↓ independently compute

Action_A(t)
Action_B(t)

↓ both committed

VALIDATE

↓ simultaneously apply

PHYSICS(t → t+1)

↓

STATE(t+1)
```

No ambiguity.

This is the heart of fairness.

---

### 4. Match adaptation

This is currently one of the most important unresolved design choices.

There are three possible models:

```text
A. Fully sealed static policy

B. Sealed code + mutable internal state

C. Recommit policy between rounds
```

I think the best RoboBladez model is:

```text
MATCH POLICY CODE
locked

PRIVATE MATCH MEMORY
mutable through committed code

ROUND-BOUNDARY MEMORY
allowed

POLICY REWRITE
not allowed until next match
```

Then a 5-round match actually tests:

> how good a learning machine did you submit?

not:

> who has a better LLM making fresh decisions between rounds?

That needs a full spec.

---

### 5. Policy sandbox determinism

This will matter massively later.

Need to define:

```text
Can policies use random?
```

Probably yes only through:

```text
policy_rng
```

seeded by the engine.

Not:

```python
random.random()
os.urandom()
time.time()
```

Otherwise rerunning a canonical match may fail.

You need:

```text
deterministic SDK
no wall clock
no network
no nondeterministic syscalls
limited CPU
limited memory
```

Very important.

---

### 6. Body design space

Right now `BladeSpec` is still basically a handful of scalars.

Eventually you need a true **body grammar**.

Something like:

```text
BladeBody
├── core
├── ring
├── contact surfaces
├── tip
├── mass distribution
└── actuator package
```

Potential design parameters:

```text
total mass
radius
radial mass profile
moment of inertia
contact polygon
contact friction
restitution
tip friction
tip geometry
height
center of mass
spin direction
actuator strength
energy capacity
```

Then enforce a construction budget.

Otherwise every body eventually converges to:

```text
max everything
```

So you need a constrained design economy:

```text
mass budget
volume budget
actuator budget
energy budget
ability budget
```

This is a major missing mechanism.

---

### 7. Body evolution legality

When can a blade change?

Possible clean model:

```text
WITHIN MATCH
body frozen

BETWEEN MATCHES
minor tuning only

BETWEEN SEASONS
major redesign allowed
```

Or:

```text
evolution points earned
```

Need to choose.

Otherwise persistent identity becomes meaningless because Boris can become an entirely different body every fight.

---

### 8. Abilities / “superpowers”

You have fragments of this in `q2`, but it needs a modern rewrite.

Every ability needs:

```text
AbilitySpec

id
version
activation_condition
energy_cost
cooldown
duration
target
state_transition
counterplay
telemetry
visual_projection
legality
```

Example:

```text
PHASE SHIFT

mechanical:
collision response multiplier → 0 for 100ms

cost:
30 energy

cooldown:
8 seconds

constraint:
cannot activate within rim zone

counterplay:
opponent can predict activation window
```

Not:

> quantum tunneling because Qiskit says so.

The fictional explanation can still be quantum.

The mechanic stays explicit.

---

### 9. Ability acquisition

This is separate from abilities themselves.

Critical question:

> Where do new abilities come from?

Possible models:

```text
A. fixed ability library
B. evolution unlock tree
C. agent proposes new ability, engine compiler validates it
D. signature move eventually promotes into legal ability
```

C is potentially incredibly interesting.

Agent says:

```json
{
  "proposed_ability": {
    "effect": "temporary friction reduction",
    "duration": 0.15,
    "energy_cost": 25
  }
}
```

Then an **Ability Compiler** maps it into legal bounded mechanics.

That gives creative evolution without letting an LLM rewrite physics.

This deserves a whole subsystem.

---

### 10. Signature moves

This mechanism needs more rigor than “cluster patterns.”

Define:

```text
candidate window
decisive delta
minimum support
cross-opponent robustness
novelty threshold
false-discovery threshold
stability period
```

A real signature should require:

```text
recurring pattern
+
successful outcome
+
multiple opponents
+
statistical support
```

Otherwise every random combo becomes a “signature.”

---

### 11. Daimon stabilization

Likewise:

```text
when does the daimon become real in canon?
```

Need exact thresholds.

Example:

```text
minimum 20 matches
dominant affinity > .40
dominant affinity stable for 10 matches
phenotype drift < threshold
signature consistency > threshold
```

Then:

```text
DAIMON_MANIFESTED
```

becomes a canonical career event.

That could be awesome.

---

### 12. Daimon evolution

After manifestation, can it change?

Probably yes.

Potential stages:

```text
LATENT
↓
EMERGING
↓
MANIFEST
↓
DEVELOPED
↓
ASCENDED
```

But these need to correspond to measurable milestones, not anime power creep.

---

### 13. Character personality

You still need to specify how much is seeded versus inferred.

I would do:

```text
GENESIS PRIORS

risk_tolerance
exploration
aggression
patience
adaptation_rate
resource_patience
social style

↓

ACTUAL HISTORY

↓

OBSERVED CHARACTER
```

So the seed nudges.

History determines.

---

### 14. Opponent model

This needs a precise data structure.

For example:

```text
OpponentModel

opponent_id

opening_distribution
response_to_center_pressure
response_to_rim_pressure
boost_usage
ability_timing
round_adaptation
body_preferences
policy_family_probs

confidence
sample_size
last_updated
```

This is one of the most important mechanisms because it produces the recursive game theory.

---

### 15. Strategic deception

This is potentially a whole unique game mechanism.

Need to decide whether an agent can deliberately condition its public history.

Example:

```text
Boris shows defensive tendencies
for 12 matches
↓
Morty models Boris as defensive
↓
Boris switches aggressive in final
```

That's emergent bluffing.

Your history system must not inadvertently leak private future strategy.

---

### 16. Information-gathering actions

We discussed sacrificial/probing rounds.

That deserves explicit support.

Not a special “probe” action necessarily.

Rather the payoff structure should allow:

```text
short-term loss
in exchange for
information gain
```

Then analysis can identify it afterward.

Potential metric:

```text
expected_information_gain
```

You could literally measure posterior reduction in opponent-policy uncertainty.

That is sick.

---

### 17. Meta analysis

You need more than Elo.

Mechanisms:

```text
head-to-head matrix
policy archetype clustering
counter relation
meta share
best response
exploitability proxy
Alpha-Rank
PSRO population
```

Then canonical events like:

```text
META_SHIFT
COUNTER_DISCOVERED
DOMINANT_STRATEGY_COLLAPSED
```

can emerge.

---

### 18. Season rules

Under-specced.

Need to decide:

```text
How often can bodies change?
How often can policies change?
Can ability sets change?
Can agents train on current-season matches?
When are patches allowed?
What happens to old engine versions?
```

I like:

```text
SEASON
rules frozen

MATCH TO MATCH
policy evolution allowed

BODY
minor tuning window

OFF-SEASON
major body evolution + ruleset changes
```

This makes eras meaningful.

---

### 19. League economy / resource constraints

Even if there's no money economy, competitors need scarce resources or trade-offs.

Could be:

```text
design budget
ability slots
body mass
energy capacity
actuator power
computation budget
```

This prevents trivial optimization.

---

### 20. Engine patching

Extremely important for a persistent sport.

Suppose v1.4 changes collision friction.

Historical match:

```text
RB-S01-M027
```

must remain:

```text
engine_version = 1.3
```

Replay it using 1.3.

New matches use 1.4.

Never “fix” history.

This needs a compatibility/versioning mechanism.

---

### 21. Canon reducers

You have events, but you need formal reducers:

```text
MATCH_ENDED
→ win/loss updated

POLICY_VERSION_CREATED
→ agent current_policy pointer

DAIMON_MANIFESTED
→ daimon canonical state

SIGNATURE_CONFIRMED
→ signature history

RIVALRY_CHANGED
→ relationship state
```

The current state should be derivable from event history.

That's a big architecture improvement.

---

### 22. Rivalries/relationships

This should not just be LLM storytelling.

Measure:

```text
match frequency
upsets
close matches
counter relationship
adaptation targeted at opponent
history length
stakes
```

Then:

```text
rivalry_score
```

can produce a canonical relationship.

LLM can name/dramatize it later.

---

### 23. Significance detection

For content:

```text
upset
record
new signature
new daimon
meta shift
first counter
long streak broken
sacrificial strategy discovered
reverse sweep
mechanically disadvantaged victory
```

These should be deterministic detectors first.

Then the story model interprets them.

---

### 24. Raw replay format

Needs a formal protocol.

Something like:

```text
RBZ Replay v1

header
engine_version
ruleset
seed
arena
body manifests
policy commitments

rounds
  frames
  actions
  events

result
digest
```

And ideally a binary/compressed representation eventually.

---

### 25. Spectator reveal levels

This is actually a product/game mechanic.

During match:

```text
show action?
show policy?
show opponent model?
```

Probably:

```text
LIVE
telemetry only

POST-MATCH
policy reveal

ANALYSIS
